\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces The benchmark SoC as its SDC and RTL define it, generated from both rather than drawn by hand. Five mutually asynchronous masters, each peripheral domain built as master $\rightarrow $ gate $\rightarrow $ divider; every peripheral keeps its AXI-Lite side in the core domain and its functional side in its own, so each dashed line is a real clock-domain crossing. Outlined in red are the three blocks that own a violating path, each labelled with the module inside it that carries the delay, the clock it fails on and its baseline slack.}}{2}{figure.caption.1}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{fig:soc}{{1}{2}{The benchmark SoC as its SDC and RTL define it, generated from both rather than drawn by hand. Five mutually asynchronous masters, each peripheral domain built as master $\rightarrow $ gate $\rightarrow $ divider; every peripheral keeps its AXI-Lite side in the core domain and its functional side in its own, so each dashed line is a real clock-domain crossing. Outlined in red are the three blocks that own a violating path, each labelled with the module inside it that carries the delay, the clock it fails on and its baseline slack}{figure.caption.1}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {2.1}Why it is shaped this way}{2}{subsection.2.1}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {2.2}The clock tree}{2}{subsection.2.2}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces The five clock domains, one chain each, read out of \texttt  {nebula.sdc}. Each dashed box is one \texttt  {-asynchronous} group: nothing inside it is timed against anything inside another. The core master drives its generated clocks directly; the four peripheral masters each run through a latch-based clock gate and a handover divider, whose internal clocks are declared and joined to the same group rather than left for the analyser to guess at.}}{3}{figure.caption.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:clocktree}{{2}{3}{The five clock domains, one chain each, read out of \texttt {nebula.sdc}. Each dashed box is one \texttt {-asynchronous} group: nothing inside it is timed against anything inside another. The core master drives its generated clocks directly; the four peripheral masters each run through a latch-based clock gate and a handover divider, whose internal clocks are declared and joined to the same group rather than left for the analyser to guess at}{figure.caption.2}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Every clock the design declares. The five masters are mutually asynchronous; each generated clock names the source it is derived from, so the analyser follows the gate and divider chain instead of treating their outputs as unclocked nets.}}{3}{table.caption.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:clocks}{{1}{3}{Every clock the design declares. The five masters are mutually asynchronous; each generated clock names the source it is derived from, so the analyser follows the gate and divider chain instead of treating their outputs as unclocked nets}{table.caption.3}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {2.3}The clock structures, and the two bugs that were in them}{4}{subsection.2.3}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces The clock-divider mux before and after the repair, with the pulse widths a simulation measured on each. The traces are schematic; the counts beside them are the testbench's, and the upper row is the original RTL run through the identical stimulus.}}{4}{figure.caption.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:runt}{{3}{4}{The clock-divider mux before and after the repair, with the pulse widths a simulation measured on each. The traces are schematic; the counts beside them are the testbench's, and the upper row is the original RTL run through the identical stimulus}{figure.caption.4}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {2.4}Scale}{4}{subsection.2.4}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces The clock-structure audit of the benchmark. Both structures started glitchy -- a bare AND clock gate and a combinational clock mux -- and both were rewritten: the gate into a latch-based ICG, the divider select into a handover mux whose branches are enabled by flops on their own clock. Simulation agrees with the audit: 0 runt pulses on the fixed structures against 8 on the originals under the same stimulus, which switches the divider select while the selected clock is high.}}{5}{table.caption.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:clockrisk}{{2}{5}{The clock-structure audit of the benchmark. Both structures started glitchy -- a bare AND clock gate and a combinational clock mux -- and both were rewritten: the gate into a latch-based ICG, the divider select into a handover mux whose branches are enabled by flops on their own clock. Simulation agrees with the audit: 0 runt pulses on the fixed structures against 8 on the originals under the same stimulus, which switches the divider select while the selected clock is high}{table.caption.5}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces The benchmark at the scale the competition asks for, measured on the routed baseline rather than estimated from the RTL.}}{5}{table.caption.6}\protected@file@percent }
